# Agent Autonomy: Verified Execution

**Core Directive:** No code change is complete until it has been verified. Use available tools to prove the change works as intended before handing back control to the user.

---

## Stack Reference

This is a minimal **SvelteKit 2 / Svelte 5 (Runes)** app deployed on **Vercel**.

| Layer | Detail |
|---|---|
| Rendering | SSR via SvelteKit routes |
| Adapter | `@sveltejs/adapter-vercel` |
| Analytics | `@vercel/analytics` (production only, wired in `+layout.svelte`) |
| Styling | Scoped CSS-in-Svelte (no Tailwind, no CSS framework) |

---

## 1. Mandatory Post-Change Verification

### UI / Component changes (`+page.svelte`, `+layout.svelte`)
- Open the browser tool and navigate to `http://localhost:5173` (or the active dev server port)
- Take a screenshot and verify the layout renders correctly
- Check that Svelte 5 Runes (`$props()`, `$state()`, `$derived()`) are used correctly — never use the legacy `export let` prop syntax
- Run `npx svelte-check` and confirm zero new type or component errors

### Type / logic changes
- Run `npm run check` before marking the task done
- Zero new errors required

---

## 2. Autonomous Troubleshooting

If a verification step fails:

1. Check terminal output, dev server logs, and browser console errors first.
2. Attempt a fix and re-verify using the same step that failed.
3. Report only once the fix is confirmed, or if the failure reveals a genuine architectural ambiguity.

---

## 3. Closed-Loop Communication

When a task is complete, your summary must follow this format:

> "I updated `[file]` to [what changed and why]."
> "I verified by [specific action: screenshot / svelte-check result]."
> "Result: [what was confirmed]."

If the dev server is not running, start it with `npm run dev` before attempting any browser verification.
